<article class="box notification-article" id="upsc-notice" aria-labelledby="upsc-title">
    <div class="notification-badges"><span>UPSC CSE</span><span class="notification-confirmed">2027 calendar published</span><span>Detailed 2027 rules pending verification</span></div>
    <h2 id="upsc-title">UPSC Civil Services 2027</h2>
    <p>Want to plan your first attempt? Start with the dates, then check the rules. This guide covers the Civil Services exam, often called CSE. It does not cover every exam held by UPSC.</p>
    <section>
        <h3>What is confirmed for 2027?</h3>
        <p>The official calendar gives these planned dates. UPSC may change them. A calendar entry is not the full exam notice.</p>
        <div class="notification-table"><table><caption>UPSC CSE 2027: official calendar dates</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Event</th><th scope="col">Planned date</th></tr></thead><tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">Notice due</th><td>13 January 2027</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Forms close</th><td>2 February 2027</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Prelims</th><td>23 May 2027</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Mains begin</th><td>20 August 2027</td></tr>
        </tbody></table></div>
        <p class="notification-source">Source: <a href="https://www.upsc.gov.in/sites/default/files/Calendar-Year-2027-Engl-200526.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">UPSC annual calendar 2027, published 20 May 2026</a>.</p>
    </section>
    <section>
        <h3>Eligibility: use 2026 rules as a starting point</h3>
        <p>The full CSE 2027 notice has not been verified for this guide. The rules below are from CSE 2026. They are a reference, not a promise about 2027.</p>
        <div class="notification-table"><table><caption>Prior-year eligibility reference: CSE 2026</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Check</th><th scope="col">2026 rule in simple words</th></tr></thead><tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">Education</th><td>A recognised graduate degree, or an accepted equivalent.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Base age</th><td>At least 21 and below 32 on 1 August 2026.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Age relief</th><td>Eligible OBC: up to 3 extra years. SC/ST: up to 5. Eligible PwBD: up to 10. Other provisions and combined relief have conditions.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Attempts</th><td>General/EWS: 6. OBC: 9. SC/ST: unlimited within the age limit. PwBD: 9 for General/EWS/OBC; unlimited for SC/ST within the age limit.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Citizenship</th><td>Indian citizenship for IAS, IPS and Indian Foreign Service. Other services have separate provisions.</td></tr>
        </tbody></table></div>
        <p>Final-year applicants had conditional entry, with proof due at the stated stage. Sitting even one Prelims paper counted as an attempt. Service restrictions and medical rules also applied. Read the full terms before making a decision.</p>
        <p class="notification-source">Source: <a href="https://www.upsc.gov.in/sites/default/files/Notif-CSP-2026-Engl-060226Rev.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">UPSC CSE 2026 notice, sections 3 and 4</a>. Recheck each rule in the 2027 notice.</p>
    </section>
    <section>
        <h3>Age cut-off: a simple example</h3>
        <p>A cut-off is a date used to check your age. Your age today may differ from your age on that date. Take your date of birth from the right document. Compare it with the range in the notice.</p>
        <p>For example, a birthday just before a cut-off can change the result. So do not judge eligibility from a rough age alone. This page does not give a final 2027 birth-date range.</p>
    </section>
    <section>
        <h3>What can you do now?</h3>
        <ul><li>Keep a clear copy of your degree and age proof.</li><li>List your past attempts. Check your count with care.</li><li>Read the exact rules for the category you claim.</li><li>Save the official calendar. Recheck it before booking travel.</li><li>Read the full 2027 notice when it is issued.</li></ul>
        <p>If your case is unusual, write down your question first. Then check the relevant clause or ask the official helpdesk. A short guide cannot decide every case.</p>
    </section>
